+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Is it better to sell your house first, then buy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Not for everyone. Sell first if you need the money from your sale to buy. Buy first if you can pay for two homes for about 3 months. Owners here split about evenly between the two, and about a third did both within a week."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you close on both houses on the same day?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. About 1 in 9 Horry County owners who sold one home and bought another here had both deeds recorded on the same day."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a kick-out clause in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"South Carolina's forms use no such name. The closest thing is the addendum for an offer that depends on a sale (SCR504) and its notice (SCR505). When an offer comes in that does not depend on another sale, the first buyer must choose within the days agreed. The SC REALTORS' legal staff say agents sometimes call the addendum a right of first refusal or a knockout clause."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How long can a seller rent back after closing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"As long as the buyer agrees in writing. A buyer whose loan is sold to Fannie Mae and who will live in the home must move in within 60 days of signing the mortgage. A longer rent-back does not fit that rule."}}]}</script>
