line-height:1.45;padding:.2rem .8rem .85rem}.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><style>section[style*="background:var(--ivory)"] .c3ph-card{background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-cards{max-width:920px}.c3ph-card strong{font-family:var(--sans)}.c3ph-card span.c3ph-t{font-size:1rem!important}.c3ph-card strong{font-size:1rem!important}ul[role=list] li span[style*="font-size:.9rem"]{font-size:1rem!important}section#c3-photo{padding-top:0}section:has(+section#c3-photo){background:var(--ivory-2)!important;padding-bottom:2rem}.c3ph-hero{margin:0}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero{max-width:900px;margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem}.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:auto!important;height:auto}.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:640px}.c3-aerial-ctop .c3ph-cr{top:0;bottom:auto;padding:.35rem .6rem 1.6rem 2.6rem;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 0%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0))}@media (max-width:699px){.c3ph-cards>:last-child:nth-child(odd){grid-column:1/-1}}.c3-maps{display:grid;gap:0 1.5rem;max-width:1136px}@media (min-width:900px){.c3-maps{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;align-items:start}}.c3-maps figure{max-width:none!important;margin:1.4rem 0 .4rem!important}.c3-maps svg,.c3-maps img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto}.c3-map-links{row-gap:0!important}.c3-map-links a{padding:.55rem 0}ul[role=list][style*="minmax(13rem,1fr)"]{grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fill,minmax(min(13rem,100%),1fr))!important}</style><style>.c3-aerial .c3ph-hero,.c3-aerial-close .c3ph-hero{max-width:720px}</style><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each way has its own cost: two homes at once, some months with no home of your own, or a purchase that depends on your sale. The times are for the middle owner: half took longer, and half took less time.</p><ul class="sb-cards" aria-label="Three ways to sell one home and buy the next"><li><span class="k">Buy first</span><b>Fits if you can pay for two homes for a while</b><dl><div><dt>How long</dt><dd><p>About 3 months owning both homes, for the middle owner here. About 1 in 5 owned both for 6 months to a year.</p></dd></div><div><dt>The risk</dt><dd><p>Two sets of house bills if your old home sells slowly.</p></dd></div><div><dt>What helps</dt><dd><p>A lender's approval with <a href="#lender-rules" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">both homes counted</a>, or cash.</p></dd></div><div><dt>Owners here</dt><dd><p>About a third bought first. Among owners who paid more for the next home, about 4 in 10 did.</p></dd></div></dl></li><li><span class="k">Sell first</span><b>Fits if you need the money from your sale to buy</b><dl><div><dt>How long</dt><dd><p>About 2 months between homes, for the middle owner here. More than 1 in 4 bought the next home within a month of the sale; about 1 in 5 took more than 6 months.</p></dd></div><div><dt>The risk</dt><dd><p>Moving twice, and paying for a place to live in between.</p></dd></div><div><dt>What helps</dt><dd><p>A <a href="#rent-back-rules" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rent-back</a>, where you stay in the house for a few weeks after closing. A <a href="/sell/cash-offer/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">cash buyer</a> can close sooner.</p></dd></div><div><dt>Owners here</dt><dd><p>About a third sold first. Among owners who bought a new home from a builder, more than 4 in 10 did.</p></dd></div></dl></li><li><span class="k">Both within a week</span><b>Fits if your sale must close before, or as, you buy</b><dl><div><dt>How long</dt><dd><p>Both closings within 7 days of each other. About 1 in 9 owners here recorded both deeds on the same day.</p></dd></div><div><dt>The risk</dt><dd><p>If your sale does not close by the date in your offer, either side can end your purchase.</p></dd></div><div><dt>What helps</dt><dd><p>An <a href="#sale-steps" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">offer that depends on your sale</a>.</p></dd></div><div><dt>Owners here</dt><dd><p>About a third did both within a week. Among owners moving to a home that cost the same or less, about 4 in 10 did.</p></dd></div></dl></li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We know the date of each sale and each purchase from county deeds, but not where owners lived in between.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="what-owners-did"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What owners did">What do Horry County owners do: sell first or buy first?</h2><div class="sb-side"><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">More of them now sell and buy within a week of each other. About 1 in 5 did in late 2021, and almost 4 in 10 did in 2025.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We read Horry County's deed records and matched the seller's name on each home sale to the buyer's name on another home deed within a year.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/horry-county-government-center-conway-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/horry-county-government-center-conway-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/horry-county-government-center-conway-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="675" alt="Horry County's brick government and justice center in Conway, with a white pillared entrance between two wings" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia. License: CC BY-SA 3.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 3.0">Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia</a></span></div><figcaption>Horry County's government and justice center in Conway, where county deeds are recorded (photo 2006).</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We kept the 1,749 owners who still own the next home and live in it. The county taxes it at the lower 4 percent rate for a home the owner lives in, called a legal residence.</p></div><figure class="sb-fig sb-stack"><p class="cap" id="sbStackCap">What owners here did, by year</p><p class="key" aria-hidden="true"><span><i class="sw w"></i>Both within a week</span><span><i class="sw b"></i>Bought first</span><span><i class="sw f"></i>Sold first</span></p><table aria-labelledby="sbStackCap"><thead class="sr"><tr><th scope="col">Year of the sale</th><th scope="col">Both within a week</th><th scope="col">Bought first</th><th scope="col">Sold first</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">2021<small>Jul to Dec</small></th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:18.9">19%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:33.7">34%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:47.3">47%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2022</th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:30">30%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:31.3">31%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:38.6">39%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2023</th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:34.5">35%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:32.7">33%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:32.7">33%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2024</th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:38.5">39%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:36.1">36%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:25.4">25%</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2025<small>Jan to Oct</small></th><td class="s w" style="flex-grow:38">38%</td><td class="s b" style="flex-grow:35.8">36%</td><td class="s f" style="flex-grow:26.2">26%</td></tr></tbody></table><figcaption>Owners who sold one Horry County home and bought another here within a year, by the year of the sale. Our count of county deeds: 1,749 owners in all.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Selling first fell from almost half of these moves to about 1 in 4. Buying first stayed near a third. The deeds do not say why.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Two in three of these owners paid more for their next home. The middle one cost about 16 percent more than the home they sold. The <a href="/homes/map/?type=house" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">houses for sale now</a> are on our map.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About a third bought a new home from a builder. Our <a href="/buyers/new-construction/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">new construction page</a> covers builders and their contracts.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Have us match your two closing dates</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Tell us the order you want. We set your listing date and your offer terms so the sale and the purchase close in that order.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us match my closing dates</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="how-long"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="How long it takes">How long does it take to sell a house in Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 3 months from listing to closing in the Myrtle Beach area, over the past year. The middle home took about 8 weeks to get a signed contract, then about 5 weeks to close.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">That is about a month longer than the national figure. Owners here who bought first owned two homes for about the same time.</p><figure class="sb-fig sb-time"><p class="cap">How long each part takes, in days</p><div role="img" aria-label="Selling a home in the Myrtle Beach area: about 8 weeks to a signed contract, then about 5 weeks to closing, about 3 months in all. Owners who bought first owned two homes for about 3 months; owners who sold first were between homes for about 2 months; the rest did both within 7 days."><div class="ln sale"><p class="who"><b>Selling a home here</b><span>about 3 months</span></p><span class="tr" aria-hidden="true"><span class="s c" style="width:57.5%"></span><span class="s k" style="left:57.5%;width:37.2%"></span></span></div><p class="key"><span><i class="sw c"></i>To a signed contract: about 8 weeks</span><span><i class="sw k"></i>Then to closing: about 5 weeks</span></p><div class="ln"><p class="who"><b>Buy first: owning two homes</b><span>about 3 months</span></p><span class="tr" aria-hidden="true"><span class="s b" style="width:91%"></span></span></div><div class="ln"><p class="who"><b>Sell first: between homes</b><span>about 2 months</span></p><span class="tr" aria-hidden="true"><span class="s f" style="width:65.5%"></span></span></div><div class="ln"><p class="who"><b>Both within a week</b><span>7 days or fewer</span></p><span class="tr" aria-hidden="true"><span class="s w" style="width:7%"></span></span></div><p class="ax"><span>0</span><span>30 days</span><span>60 days</span><span>90 days</span></p></div><figcaption>Selling: Zillow's figures for the Myrtle Beach area over the 12 months to September 2026. The three ways: the middle owner in our count of county deeds.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The month you list changes the first part. Our page on <a href="/sell/best-time-to-sell/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">the best time to sell</a> gives it month by month.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="buy-first"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Buying first">Can you buy a house before you sell yours?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, if a lender approves you with both homes counted, or you pay cash. Many regular home loans are sold to Fannie Mae, and its rules say what the lender counts:</p><ul class="sb-plain" id="lender-rules" aria-label="What a lender counts when you buy before you sell"><li><b>The costs of both homes</b><p>If your home is under contract but will not close before the new loan, the lender counts <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-6-06/qualifying-impact-other-real-estate-owned" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">both homes' costs</a>. Those are the payment, taxes, insurance and any HOA dues. It can leave the old home's cost out once you show the signed sale contract and that your buyer's loan condition has been cleared. That condition lets your buyer back out if their loan is not approved.</p></li><li><b>If you keep the old home</b><p>If you keep it as a second home, its costs count too. If you rent it out, the lender's rules on rental income say how much of the rent counts. Our page on <a href="/sell/rental-property/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental property</a> covers renting it out or selling it later.</p></li><li><b>A bridge loan</b><p>A bridge loan is a short loan to help you buy before you sell. If your new home loan is sold to Fannie Mae, a <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-4.3-14/bridgeswing-loans" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">bridge loan</a> can help pay for the purchase. It cannot be secured by the new home. The lender must document that you can make the payments on the new home, the current home, the bridge loan and your other debts.</p></li><li><b>Who decides</b><p>Whether you qualify, and on what terms, is the lender's decision. A home equity line of credit (HELOC) is another loan against your home, and the lender decides on that too.</p></li></ul><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Find out if a lender will count both homes</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We put you in touch with a loan officer at our preferred lender, who can tell you what you qualify for with both homes counted.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us connect you with a lender</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="sale-contingency"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Depending on your sale">How does an offer that depends on selling your house work in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">You add an addendum to the purchase contract. In South Carolina, an offer that depends on the buyer selling another home adds the SC REALTORS <a href="https://screaltors.org/scr504-sale-of-buyers-property-contingency/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">sale-contingency addendum</a> (SCR504) to the purchase contract.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Agents call it a sale contingency. The purchase goes ahead only if your own sale closes in time.</p><ol class="sb-steps" id="sale-steps" aria-label="How an offer that depends on selling your home works in South Carolina"><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">1</span><span class="c"><b>The deadline</b>The addendum gives the date by which your own home must sell and close. If that date is missed, either side can end the contract by written notice within the days the addendum gives.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">2</span><span class="c"><b>While you wait</b>You must make a good-faith effort to sell your home, and keep the seller informed about that sale.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">3</span><span class="c"><b>The seller keeps showing the house</b>The seller can keep showing the house and accept a backup offer, a second offer that moves up if yours ends.</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">4</span><span class="c"><b>The notice</b>If an offer comes in that does not depend on another sale, the seller can send you a notice (SCR505).</span></li><li><span class="n" aria-hidden="true">5</span><span class="c"><b>Your choice</b>Within the days agreed, you choose one of four things. You can show that your home is under contract to close in time. You can drop the condition if you can buy without selling. You can step back to second place, or end the contract.</span></li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We write the days into the addendum with you. The closing attorney can explain what they mean for your own purchase.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">When you are the seller, a buyer's offer can come with the same addendum. The same steps then apply from the other side.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="rent-back"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Rent-back">Can you sell your house and stay after closing?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, if your buyer agrees in writing. This is called a rent-back: you stay in the house after closing and pay the buyer for the days you stay.</p><ul class="sb-plain" id="rent-back-rules" aria-label="Staying in the house after closing"><li><b>The standard contract</b><p>Under the South Carolina REALTORS contract, the seller hands over the house empty and reasonably clean at closing, with all keys, codes and remotes. That changes only if seller and buyer agree otherwise in writing.</p></li><li><b>The buyer's loan</b><p>A buyer with a loan sold to Fannie Mae who will live in the home must <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-1.1-01/occupancy-types" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">move in within 60 days</a> of signing the mortgage. A rent-back longer than 60 days does not fit that rule. Cash buyers and investors are outside it.</p></li><li><b>What we put in writing</b><p>The move-out day, the rent for each day, a deposit, who pays the power and water, and who insures what.</p></li></ul><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Need a few weeks in the house after you sell?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">We ask for the rent-back in your contract, so the days and the rent are agreed before closing day.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Have us ask for a rent-back</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="taxes"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Property taxes">What happens to your property taxes if you own two homes for a while?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A home that was your legal residence for any part of a year keeps the <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=12&chapter=43" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">4 percent rate</a> for that whole year. A home that is no longer your legal residence is taxed at 6 percent.</p><ul class="sb-plain" aria-label="Property tax and income tax when you own two homes"><li><b>When you move out</b><p>The law asks you to tell the county assessor within six months of the change.</p></li><li><b>The new home</b><p>You apply for the 4 percent rate by January 15 of the year after the first tax year you claim it. You certify that no one in your household claims it on another home. Our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">property tax page</a> has the rates in dollars.</p></li><li><b>If you rent out the old home</b><p>The federal tax break on a home sale needs <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc701" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2 years lived there</a> out of the 5 before the sale. If you lived there at least 2 years, you have up to 3 years after moving out to sell and still meet that test. Our <a href="/sell/capital-gains/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">capital gains page</a> covers the rest of the rule. A tax pro can tell you how it applies to your sale.</p></li></ul></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="sell-or-buy-first-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Sell or buy first FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is it better to sell your house first, then buy?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Not for everyone. Sell first if you need the money from your sale to buy. Buy first if you can pay for two homes for about 3 months. Owners here split about evenly between the two, and about a third did both within a week.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you close on both houses on the same day?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. About 1 in 9 Horry County owners who sold one home and bought another here had both deeds recorded on the same day.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is a kick-out clause in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina's forms use no such name. The closest thing is the addendum for an offer that depends on a sale (SCR504) and its notice (SCR505). When an offer comes in that does not depend on another sale, the first buyer must choose within the days agreed. The SC REALTORS' legal staff say agents sometimes call the addendum a right of first refusal or a knockout clause.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How long can a seller rent back after closing?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">As long as the buyer agrees in writing. A buyer whose loan is sold to Fannie Mae and who will live in the home must move in within 60 days of signing the mortgage. A longer rent-back does not fit that rule.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County deeds</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycounty.org/parcelapp/rest/services/HorryCountyGISApp/MapServer/24" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">County parcels</a>, <a href="https://www.zillow.com/research/data/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zillow Research</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-6-06/qualifying-impact-other-real-estate-owned" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B3-6-06</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b3-4.3-14/bridgeswing-loans" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B3-4.3-14</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b2-1.1-01/occupancy-types" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B2-1.1-01</a>, <a href="https://screaltors.org/scr504-sale-of-buyers-property-contingency/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC REALTORS on SCR504</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/statmast.php?title=12&chapter=43" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc701" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Topic 701</a>. What owners did and how long it took: our count of county deeds, sales recorded July 2021 to October 2025. Sale times: Zillow, the Myrtle Beach area.</p><div class="sb-notes"><p class="sb-small"><strong>How we counted.</strong> We read 55,081 home resales recorded in Horry County from July 2021 to October 2025. 34,108 had one person as the first-named seller, named with a first name, a middle name or initial and a last name. Companies, trusts and estates were left out. We also left out names on more than 4 home deeds, such as investors and common names, which left 33,089.</p><p class="sb-small">For 3,233 of those sales, the same name bought another Horry County home within a year before or after. 1,749 of those buyers still own the new home, and the county taxes it at the 4 percent rate for a home the owner lives in. The true number of moves is higher. A couple named in a different order on the two deeds is missed, and so is a purchase in a trust's name.</p><p class="sb-small">Dates are the days the county recorded each deed. Days to a contract and to closing are Zillow's figures for the Myrtle Beach area.</p><p class="sb-small"><strong>Legal notice.</strong> Chapter3 Realty Corp is a licensed South Carolina real estate brokerage. We are not attorneys, lenders or tax advisers, and nothing on this page is legal, loan or tax advice.</p></div><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Horry County's brick government and justice center in Conway, with a white pillared entrance between two wings. Photo: Pollinator at English Wikipedia, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped and resized the photo.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/horry-county-government-center-conway-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-n/sell-sell-or-buy-first/horry-county-government-center-conway-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Horry County's brick government and justice center in Conway, with a white pillared entrance between two wings","caption":"Horry County's government and justice center in Conway, where county deeds are recorded (photo 2006).","description":"Horry County's brick government and justice center in Conway, with a white pillared entrance between two wings","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Pollinator at English Wikipedia"},"creditText":"Pollinator, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"© Pollinator at English Wikipedia, CC BY-SA 3.0","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"1301 Second Avenue, Conway, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Have us plan your move from one home to the next</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Tell us when you want to be in the next home. We plan your listing, your offer and both closings back from that day.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Have us plan my move</a></div></section></main>
